WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:21.040
This is the head of the World Health
Organization Dr Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.520
Earlier this year he DM'd me on
Twitter. He said I should come to the DRC

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.940
and see for myself how complicated Ebola
was to control there.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:37.380
A few months later I was flying to the Eastern DRC.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.620
One of the first things I noticed once I landed

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:45.409
was the presence of tanks and soldiers.
There's been decades of conflict in the

00:00:45.409 --> 00:00:50.089
area and war and political instability
are really at the root of why Ebola

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:51.980
has been so hard to contain.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.240
This is a photo outside of an Ebola centre. There's

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.710
soldiers stationed behind sandbags at
the entrance. The reason they're there is

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.979
that this particular Ebola center had
already been attacked twice, had been

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:08.330
burnt to the ground. But at the same time
the presence of soldiers might be

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:13.369
intimidating for some people. And that's
on top of the fact that Ebola is a

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:18.429
terrifying disease and then the response
to Ebola is also strange and terrifying.

00:01:18.429 --> 00:01:22.729
Someone in this household was diagnosed
with Ebola and now there's these Ebola

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:27.259
responders dressed in these weird white
Tyvek suits who come in and just douse

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:31.039
all of somebody's belongings in chlorine.
Some people have even set up chairs to

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:33.220
watch the whole scene.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.600
But the care being offered to 
people is improving compared

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.630
to previous outbreaks.
Here's within an Ebola treatment

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:43.579
centre and there's a plastic cube around
a patient that allows family members to

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:49.100
see in and patients to see out. In
addition this time there's not only

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.700
intravenous fluids that are given to
every patient but people are getting

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.049
experimental Ebola drugs and two of
those Ebola drugs have now been proven

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:02.299
to be up to 90% effective if people get
them early. And the other bright spot is

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:07.159
that there's now an Ebola vaccine that's
been widely deployed in this outbreak

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:11.480
for the first time. So you would think
that this outbreak would be contained by

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.950
now but it's not and there's a few
reasons for that. One of them is that

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:22.100
it's difficult to know who is at high
risk. Also vaccine teams can't vaccinate

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.550
people who don't want to be vaccinated. And people aren't getting experimental

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.480
drugs if they don't go into Ebola
centres. What they'll do first is go to

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.730
the places they're used to going so that
could be a pastor, it could be a

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:40.470
traditional healer, it could be a small
pharmacy or a small clinic. These sort of

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:44.670
pharmacies are just all over the place.
There's hundreds of them. You can see

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.680
like a snake around a chalice that's
sort of an insignia.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:54.150
This is a photo inside of one of these health facilities.
This woman is feverish and rather than

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.570
have her mix with all of the other
patients that might show up there

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:02.220
they've decided to put patients who have
symptoms of Ebola in one area so that

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:08.220
they're separated from others. So in this
room a mother's sitting by her child and

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.970
the doctor thinks the child should
perhaps go to an Ebola treatment

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:15.840
centre but the mother doesn't want her
child to go. And if you can put yourself

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.200
in the mother's shoes,
the Ebola centre's about an hour away,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.520
she'd have to be separated, she can't
hold her child, she can't be there next

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.290
to him. This is the kind of mistrust or
reluctance that comes from a place of

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.300
love and concern and not conflict

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.900
This is a photo of an Ebola checkpoint. These

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:41.100
are set up to contain the outbreak. What
happens is when people go past they have

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.610
their temperature taken and if they have
a fever they're not allowed to travel

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:48.930
through a checkpoint. And these
checkpoints have been very helpful but

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:53.190
at the same time they're not bulletproof.
People with Ebola have now traveled to

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:57.600
Goma in North Kivu which is a very
international city and they've now

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.640
traveled 700 kilometers south.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.860
What all of this comes down to is that Dr Tedros

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.570
has a huge amount of work in front
of him. The world has completely ignored

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:14.400
a humanitarian crisis in the DRC for a
quarter century. That crisis is why the

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.320
Ebola outbreak has now killed more than
2,000 people and if we keep ignoring

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.220
Ebola too - it's only going to help the virus
spread.

